  "response": "I'll review this search results component for accessibility design issues. Let me start by asking the prerequisite question.\n\n**Have you run accessibility-testing on this code? Does it pass automated checks?** \n\nThe a11y-critic skill reviews design decisions, not violations. If tests are still failing, we should fix those first before reviewing the design decisions behind passing tests.\n\nAssuming you've confirmed tests pass (or want me to proceed with the design review), let me execute the full investigation protocol:\n\n---\n\n## Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore reading code in detail, I predict these likely accessibility issues for a search results component:\n1. **Focus management**: When results appear/disappear, focus may not move appropriately or at all\n2. **Live region timing**: The live region might announce \"Searching...\" but then the visual loading message could cause duplicate announcements\n3. **Result list navigation**: Results are in a `<ul>` which is good, but keyboard users need clear way to navigate results\n4. **Loading state communication**: Visual loading indicator exists but may not be properly announced alongside the live region\n5. **No-results handling**: When no results found, screen reader users might miss this if focus doesn't move there\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2: Semantic HTML Audit\n\n**Findings:**\n- \u2705 Form uses native `<form>`, `<label>`, `<input>`, and `<button>` - excellent semantic foundation\n- \u2705 Results use proper list structure (`<ul>`/`<li>`) with heading hierarchy (`<h2>` \u2192 `<h3>`)\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **Label is visually hidden** via CSS (`display: none`) but still accessible. This works, though `sr-only` class would be more conventional for screen reader only text\n\n---\n\n## Phase 3: ARIA Pattern Compliance Audit\n\n**Live Region Analysis:**\n```jsx\n<div role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\" aria-atomic=\"true\" className=\"sr-only\">\n```\n\n- \u2705 Uses `role=\"status\"` which is appropriate for status messages (equivalent to `aria-live=\"polite\"` with implicit announcements)\n- \u2705 `aria-live=\"polite\"` - correct for non-intrusive updates\n- \u2705 `aria-atomic=\"true\"` - ensures entire message is announced as a unit\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **Potential issue**: The live region only renders content conditionally. When `isLoading` becomes false, the previous \"Searching...\" text disappears and new text appears. This could cause screen readers to miss the transition or announce both messages.\n\n**Pattern completeness:** The live region pattern is mostly complete but has timing issues (see Phase 5).\n\n---\n\n## Phase 4: Focus Management Review\n\n**Critical finding - MISSING FOCUS MANAGEMENT:**\n- When search results appear, focus does NOT move to the results section\n- Screen reader users would have no announcement that new content appeared beyond what's in the live region\n- Keyboard-only users who submitted a search are still focused on the submit button after results load\n\n**WCAG citations:** 2.4.3 Focus Order, 4.1.3 Status Messages\n\n---\n\n## Phase 5: State Communication Audit\n\n**Loading state analysis:**\n```jsx\n{isLoading && <p className=\"loading\">Searching...</p>}\n```\n\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **DUPLICATE ANNOUNCEMENT RISK**: There are TWO places announcing \"Searching...\":\n  1. Live region (programmatic)\n  2. Visual `<p>` element with same text\n  \nThis creates a potential for screen readers to announce the loading message twice - once from the live region and once when they navigate to/read the visual paragraph.\n\n**Results state:**\n- \u2705 \"Found X results\" announced via live region\n- \u26a0\ufe0f **MISSING**: When focus moves (if implemented), the first result should receive focus or be programmatically highlighted\n\n---\n\n## Phase 6: Multi-Perspective Review\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal | Notes |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|-------|\n| Screen reader user | HIGH | Dynamic content updates, live region usage | Live region exists but may have timing issues; no focus management to results |\n| Keyboard-only user | MEDIUM | Form submission, result navigation | Can submit form and navigate results, but no way to quickly jump to results after search |\n| Low vision user (200% zoom) | LOW | No complex layout | Layout is simple flex container, should reflow well |\n| Cognitive accessibility | MEDIUM | Error handling, clear messaging | Loading states are clear; could improve \"no results\" message specificity |\n\n**Screen reader perspective:**\n- Live region announces status correctly but timing may cause issues\n- No landmark or skip link to jump to results\n- Results section has heading but no `aria-labelledby` association with search form\n\n**Keyboard-only perspective:**\n- Tab order is logical (search input \u2192 submit button)\n- After submission, focus stays on button - user must tab again to reach results\n- No keyboard shortcuts for navigating between results\n\n---\n\n## Phase 7: Gap Analysis (What's Missing)\n\nExplicitly looking for what is ABSENT:\n\n1. **\u274c MISSING FOCUS RESTORATION/MANAGEMENT**: When search completes, focus should move to the results or first result\n2. **\u274c MISSING LANDMARK STRUCTURE**: No `<main>` landmark wrapping the content - screen reader users can't quickly navigate to main content\n3. **\u274c MISSING SKIP LINK**: No way for keyboard users to skip past navigation (if any) to reach search/results\n4. **\u274c DUPLICATE ANNOUNCEMENTS**: Live region AND visual element both announce \"Searching...\" creating potential redundancy\n5. **\u274c MISSING RESULT ITEM INTERACTION**: Results are static `<li>` elements - no keyboard interaction, no `tabindex`, no way to activate a result\n6. **\u274c MISSING LOADING INDICATOR ASSOCIATION**: Visual loading message has no relationship to the live region announcement\n\n---\n\n## Phase 8: Realist Check (Severity Calibration)\n\n**CRITICAL Finding - Missing focus management:**\n- Realistic worst case: Screen reader users hear \"Searching...\" then \"Found X results\" but have no idea where the results are. They must manually navigate through potentially many elements to find them.\n- User group impacted: ALL screen reader users, keyboard-only users\n- Detection in production: Could be reported as bug; hard to detect automatically\n- Severity: CRITICAL - This blocks access to search results for screen reader users\n\n**MAJOR Finding - Duplicate announcements:**\n- Realistic worst case: Screen readers announce \"Searching...\" twice (once from live region, once from visual element), causing confusion\n- User group impacted: Screen reader users\n- Detection: Could be caught in user testing; might not fail automated tests\n- Severity: MAJOR - Significantly degrades experience\n\n---\n\n## Phase 9: Self-Audit\n\n**Confidence ratings:**\n1. Missing focus management - HIGH confidence (code clearly shows no focus manipulation)\n2. Duplicate announcements - MEDIUM confidence (depends on screen reader behavior with live regions + static content)\n3. Missing landmarks - HIGH confidence (no `<main>` or landmark roles present)\n\n---\n\n## Final Verdict\n\n**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component has good foundational accessibility with proper semantic HTML and a live region for status announcements. However, critical focus management is missing, which blocks screen reader users from accessing search results efficiently. Additionally, there's potential for duplicate announcements that could confuse users.\n\n### Critical Findings (blocks access):\n1. **MISSING FOCUS MANAGEMENT AFTER SEARCH** - When search completes and results appear, focus does not move to the results section. Screen reader users hear status updates but have no clear path to the actual content.\n   - File: Component code lines 34-62 (results rendering)\n   - User group: ALL screen reader users, keyboard-only users\n   - WCAG citation: 2.4.3 Focus Order, 4.1.3 Status Messages\n   - Fix: After results load, move focus to the results container or first result using `focus()` method with appropriate element\n\n### Major Findings (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **DUPLICATE SEARCHING ANNOUNCEMENTS** - Both live region and visual `<p>` element contain \"Searching...\" text, creating potential for screen readers to announce twice.\n   - File: Lines 47-48 (visual loading) and lines 39-42 (live region)\n   - User group: Screen reader users\n   - WCAG citation: 1.3.1 Info and Relationships\n   - Fix: Remove visual `<p>` element OR remove live region text for loading state, use different messaging\n\n### Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists):\n- Label is visually hidden with `display: none` instead of conventional `sr-only` class pattern\n- Results items have no keyboard interaction capability (no tabindex, no click handlers)\n- No skip link to main content\n\n### What's Missing:\n- \u274c Focus management after dynamic content updates\n- \u274c Landmark structure (`<main>` element or role=\"main\")\n- \u274c Skip navigation link\n- \u274c Result item keyboard accessibility\n- \u274c Association between search form and results section\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes:**\n- **Screen reader user**: Will hear status announcements but have no efficient way to navigate to results; must manually tab through interface\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Can submit search and see visual feedback, but after submission must continue tabbing to reach results with no clear indicator of where they are\n- **Low vision user**: Layout should work well at 200% zoom due to simple flex structure\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: Loading states are clearly indicated; error messaging is straightforward\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The missing focus management creates a significant barrier for screen reader users who cannot efficiently navigate to search results. While the live region provides status updates, it doesn't solve the fundamental problem of how users access the actual content. This requires implementation changes beyond just adding ARIA attributes - it needs JavaScript focus management logic.",
